sec.p:
The temporal dynamics of the mobile receptor pools are governed by the following system of ODEs:
(1)
aCPM,mobdt=−kint⋅CPM,mob+krec⋅CEE+krec2⋅CRE \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \frac{a C_{P M, \text { mob }}}{d t}=-k_{\text {int }} \cdot C_{P M, \text { mob }}+k_{\text {rec }} \cdot C_{E E}+k_{\text {rec } 2} \cdot C_{RE}$$\end{document}

The simulated CD98hc concentrations were mapped to the experimental readout using:
(5)
CPM, sim =CPM,mob+CPM,imm \begin{document}$$\\displaystyle C_{P M, \text { sim }}=C_{P M,mob }+C_{P M,imm}$$\end{document}

By treating the total mobile receptor pool as conserved (Ctot = CPM + CEE + CRE) and lumping together the endosomal recycling rates, the deterministic dynamics of the mobile plasma membrane pool can be analytically mapped to an Ornstein-Uhlenbeck (OU) process:
(9)
dCPM,mob=−θ(CPM,mob−μ)dt+σdWt \begin{document}$$\displaystyle d C_{P M,mob}=-\theta\left(C_{P M,mob}-\mu\right) d t+\sigma d W_{t}$$\end{document}
where dWt represents a standard Wiener process accounting for the continuous random nature of intracellular vesicular exchange at the macroscale level of a single cell, and σ represents the absolute amplitude of that intrinsic biological noise. In this OU formulation, the equilibrium set-point μ and the homeostatic restoring force θ are explicitly defined by the trafficking rates:
(10)
μ=(kt−reckint+kt−rec)Ctot \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \mu=\left(\frac{k_{t-r e c}}{k_{int}+k_{t-r e c}}\right) C_{t o t}$$\end{document}

To simulate population-level distributions of CD98hc without violating physical mass conservation, the stochastic noise was modeled as a dynamic exchange flux between the mobile plasma membrane and the early endosome. The full three-compartment SDE system was formulated as:
(12)
dCPM,mob=(−kint⋅CPM,mob+krec⋅CEE+krec2⋅CRE)dt+σdWt \begin{document}$$\displaystyle d C_{PM, mob }=\left(-k_{int} \cdot C_{PM,mob}+k_{rec} \cdot C_{E E}+k_{rec2} \cdot C_{R E}\right) d t+\sigma d W_{t}$$\end{document}
